{"items":[{"index":22,"prompt":"We’re preparing the front panel for a boxed set of conversation cards used by libraries and community groups to help strangers feel welcome. The audience is broad—families, newcomers, and people meeting across generations—so the human figures should feel inviting and varied rather than like a uniform crowd. Please build the package illustration around a regular grid of human silhouettes, using repetition and controlled variation to make the pattern feel designed rather than mechanically copied.

Use exactly 30 separate figure cells, arranged in 6 columns and 5 rows. Each cell contains one complete, recognizable human silhouette; keep every figure within its cell and leave a visible gap between neighboring figures. Let five pose families—standing, walking, seated, reaching, and greeting—cycle across each row. Alternate mirrored versions in adjacent columns, and vary body proportions and clothing outlines across rows so repeated poses don’t read as duplicates. Keep the figures upright and legible at package scale, with simple, joined shapes rather than internal facial detail. The grid should remain evident even if you choose to suggest its divisions without drawing cell borders.

Treat this as real front-of-box packaging, not a detached pattern study. Make room for the product name and a brief supporting line, but let the silhouette system carry the visual identity. Choose the palette, overall placement, and rendering style to suit the welcoming purpose." ,"skills":["procedural_geometry","counting","positioning"]}]}
